Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** to host the server in-process, then connect the official MCP C# SDK client to it via the factory's `HttpClient`.

```csharp
// 1. Create the in-memory test server
await using var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>();
var httpClient = factory.CreateClient();

// 2. Connect an MCP client through it (Streamable HTTP transport)
var transport = new SseClientTransport(
    new SseClientTransportOptions
    {
        Endpoint = new Uri(httpClient.BaseAddress!, "/mcp"),
        TransportMode = HttpTransportMode.StreamableHttp
    },
    httpClient          // ← key: passes the factory's HttpClient
);

await using var mcpClient = await McpClientFactory.CreateAsync(transport);

// 3. Test real MCP operations
var tools = await mcpClient.ListToolsAsync();
Assert.Contains(tools, t => t.Name == "my-tool");

var result = await mcpClient.CallToolAsync("my-tool", new { input = "hello" });
Assert.Equal("expected", result.Content[0].Text);
```

**Key points:**

- **`WebApplicationFactory`** starts your full middleware pipeline (auth, routing, DI) but uses an in-memory `TestServer` — no real ports.
- Pass the factory's `HttpClient` into `SseClientTransport` so all HTTP traffic stays in-process.
- Use `HttpTransportMode.StreamableHttp` or `.Sse` to match your server's transport.
- NuGet packages: `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` + `ModelContextProtocol`.

This gives you true end-to-end MCP coverage (serialization, negotiation, session handling) with the speed of a unit test.
